<p class="page-kicker">Bluetooth troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Sennheiser Momentum 4 won't pair: pairing mode first, reset later.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">Pairing mode and hardware reset are different procedures on Momentum 4. Start with the documented pairing sequence, clear stale host state, and use a reset only when the headphones themselves are not behaving correctly.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Turn the headphones off and place them near the device. Hold the multi-function button for <strong>5 seconds</strong> to enter pairing mode, then choose <strong>MOMENTUM 4</strong> from the Bluetooth list. If it was paired before, forget the old entry and retry. Test another phone/computer. If the headphones are unresponsive or functioning incorrectly, Sennheiser’s current support article documents an approximately <strong>20-second</strong> multi-function-button hold for a hardware reset.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Use the documented 5-second pairing sequence.</h2>
<p>Sennheiser’s instruction manual says to switch the headphones off, put them close to the Bluetooth device, and press the multi-function button for 5 seconds. The LEDs show a white continuous sequence and a pairing melody plays. Select <strong>MOMENTUM 4</strong> on the host.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Remove the host’s stale pairing record.</h2>
<p>If Momentum 4 is already listed but will not reconnect, forget/remove the device from the host Bluetooth settings, toggle Bluetooth off and back on, and pair again. This is less disruptive than factory-resetting the headphones.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Use Sennheiser Smart Control to inspect saved connections.</h2>
<p>Sennheiser documents a Connections function in Smart Control for selecting paired devices and removing pairing information. The headphones can save multiple profiles, so an old or unexpected active connection can matter.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Test another phone or computer.</h2>
<p>If the headphones pair normally with another device, focus on the original host’s Bluetooth stack, saved device record, updates, or radio. If they fail on every host, continue with the headphone-side reset/support path.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Use the hardware reset for malfunction, not as the first pairing step.</h2>
<p>Sennheiser’s current support guidance says to hold the multi-function button for approximately 20 seconds when the headphones are not functioning correctly; a small tick confirms restart. Factory-default reset options can also exist through Smart Control/current product support. Follow the current Sennheiser support page if the app/manual version differs.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need replacement headphones?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If forgetting/re-pairing or another host works, keep the headphones and fix the host pairing state.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If only one radio/OS fails, troubleshoot that host before replacing hardware.</p></div>
<div><strong>Support / replacement</strong><p>If Momentum 4 cannot enter pairing mode or connect to multiple known-good hosts after Sennheiser’s documented reset/support steps, contact Sennheiser.</p></div>
</div></section>

